    "id": 35,
    "stop_id": "philosophers_stone",
    "title": "Philosopher's Stone",
    "description": "Welcome to the enchanting courtyard of Collegium Maius, where legend and history intertwine around the so-called Philosopher’s Stone. Here, in the oldest part of Jagiellonian University, stories have circulated for centuries about alchemists, scholars, and the age-old quest to unlock the mysteries of nature.\n\nThe Philosopher’s Stone, as imagined in medieval and Renaissance lore, was said to have the power to transmute base metals into gold and grant eternal life. According to Kraków’s legends, it was right here in Collegium Maius that the city’s most famous alchemists-including the legendary Pan Twardowski, the “Polish Faust”-searched for this elusive artifact. Some tales even claim that Doctor Faustus himself practiced his mysterious arts within these very walls, hoping to discover the secret of the stone.\n\nThe courtyard itself is a masterpiece of late Gothic architecture, surrounded by arcaded galleries and filled with the echoes of centuries of scholarly debate. Professors and students once gathered here to discuss science, philosophy, and the mysteries of the universe, and it’s easy to imagine an alchemist quietly slipping away to a hidden corner, intent on his experiments. The university’s collection includes medieval scientific instruments and globes, reminders of the era when the boundaries between science and magic were still blurred.\n\nOver the years, the legend of the Philosopher’s Stone at Collegium Maius has inspired writers, artists, and countless visitors. It’s said that if you pause in the courtyard and let your imagination wander, you might just catch a whisper of the secrets that fascinated generations of scholars. Today, the Collegium Maius is a museum, welcoming visitors to explore its treasures and perhaps, just for a moment, feel the magic of Kraków’s alchemical past.\n\nAnd here’s a fun anecdote: local tradition holds that if you make a wish while standing by the old well in the courtyard-where some say the stone is hidden-your wish might come true, especially if it’s a wish for wisdom or inspiration.\n\nOur next stop: Palace \"Under the Rams\". A Renaissance palace from the 16th century, famous for its arcaded courtyard, artistic legacy, and the legendary Piwnica pod Baranami cabaret.",
